1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:01,280
You wake up with a fever.

2
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:04,380
There's a lump under your arm the size of an egg.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:07,520
Your neighbor is already dead.

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,020
And the smartest doctors in Europe have just announced what's causing all this.

5
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:13,240
Jupiter.

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,730
And Saturn.

7
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:16,020
And Mars.

8
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:18,040
Basically, the sky.

9
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,940
Welcome to England, 1348.

10
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,840
Today we're finding out exactly why you wouldn't survive the Black Death.

11
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:27,970
And see that little meter in the corner?

12
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:31,020
Those are your odds of making it to the end of this video.

13
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,240
Spoiler: they're going down.

14
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:40,460
But stick around until the end, because the few people who did survive got something nobody in your village could have imagined.

15
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,230
But let's rewind a year.

16
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:44,520
It's the spring of 1347.

17
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,740
You're a peasant in a small village in the south of England,

18
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:48,940
and honestly?

19
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:50,200
Life is fine.

20
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:52,280
Terrible, but fine.

21
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:54,200
You own almost nothing.

22
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,980
You owe your lord a couple of days of work in his fields every week,

23
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,820
before you're allowed to touch your own.

24
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:01,810
And dinner is bread.

25
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:04,140
With a little pottage, if things are going well.

26
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:08,500
Then the merchants at the market start talking about a sickness in the East.

27
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,420
By October 1347, it's in Sicily, arriving on ships at Messina.

28
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,500
Within months, it's in Italy.

29
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:16,620
Then France.

30
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,860
Then everywhere ships can go.

31
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:20,900
And you do what everyone does.

32
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:23,220
You assume it's somebody else's problem.

33
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:24,600
England is an island.

34
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,860
God is clearly on your side.

35
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:30,000
And Sicily is very, very far away.

36
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:32,100
It is not that far away.

37
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:37,200
In June 1348, according to a chronicle written by friars in King's Lynn,

38
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,800
two ships land at Melcombe, in Dorset.

39
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,560
On one of them is a sailor from Gascony, in France, and he's already sick.

40
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,240
The chronicle blames him.

41
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:48,940
Historians aren't so sure it was that simple.

42
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:52,240
Bristol and Southampton also have a claim to being first.

43
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,960
Nobody wins this competition.

44
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:59,050
From the coast, the plague moves the way everything moves in medieval England: along roads,

45
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:00,660
rivers and markets.

46
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:02,180
Bristol by the summer.

47
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:03,640
London by the autumn.

48
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:07,480
Over the next year, it reaches almost every corner of the country.

49
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:09,160
Including your village.

50
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:13,720
And just like that, about half the people around you are not going to make it.

51
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,200
We haven't even gotten to the bad part.

52
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:19,000
Now, you've probably heard that rats spread it.

53
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,300
That's partly true.

54
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:25,840
The disease is caused by a bacterium called Yersinia pestis, and it's carried by fleas.

55
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,520
Fleas live on rats.

56
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:33,560
But some researchers now think that in Europe, human fleas and body lice did a lot of the work,

57
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:37,360
jumping from person to person in crowded, unwashed homes.

58
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,760
And your home is very crowded.

59
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:41,640
And very unwashed.

60
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,260
You share one room with your whole family.

61
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,340
Possibly also with the animals.

62
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,340
So, let's say a flea finds you.

63
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:50,740
Here's what happens next.

64
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:52,240
First, fever.

65
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:53,100
A headache.

66
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:53,860
Chills.

67
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,900
Then a painful swelling appears in your groin, your armpit or your neck.

68
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,860
It's called a bubo, and that's where the name "bubonic" comes from.

69
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:06,940
That's the bubonic version, and believe it or not, it's the "good" one.

70
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:15,820
Even today, the World Health Organization estimates that untreated bubonic plague kills between 30 and 60 percent of the people who catch it.

71
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,960
So it's basically a coin flip.

72
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:19,340
There's a worse version.

73
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,380
If the infection reaches your lungs, it becomes pneumonic plague.

74
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:27,720
Now it spreads through coughing, person to person, no flea required.

75
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:32,260
Untreated, it's almost always fatal, sometimes within a day.

76
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:35,280
And in 1348, nothing is treated.

77
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:37,340
Because nobody knows what's happening.

78
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:38,860
Let's talk about your doctors.

79
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,400
Well, you're a peasant.

80
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:41,620
You don't have a doctor.

81
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,260
But the people who do aren't doing much better.

82
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:51,340
In 1348, the King of France asked the top medical experts at the University of Paris to explain the plague.

83
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:52,540
Their official answer?

84
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:53,680
The planets.

85
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,890
Specifically, a conjunction of Saturn, Jupiter and Mars on the 20th of March,

86
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:02,740
1345, which supposedly corrupted the air.

87
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:07,920
That idea, poisoned air, or "miasma", was the best science of the time.

88
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:11,560
So the cures actually make sense, in a terrible way.

89
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:14,800
Carry flowers or herbs, so you're breathing something nicer.

90
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:16,399
Splash vinegar around.

91
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,500
Burn something that smells strong.

92
00:04:18,779 --> 00:04:20,060
Don't bathe too much.

93
00:04:20,339 --> 00:04:25,130
The Paris experts listed bathing right next to too much exercise and too much sex,

94
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:27,560
as things that made your body more vulnerable.

95
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:31,020
Honestly, for a medieval peasant, that one wasn't hard to follow.

96
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:35,540
And of course, bloodletting, which was the medieval answer to almost everything.

97
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:37,340
Feeling weak and feverish?

98
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:38,980
Let's remove some of your blood.

99
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:46,300
Even the Pope, Clement the Sixth, in Avignon, was advised by his physician to keep fires burning to purify the air.

100
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:48,320
And to be fair, he survived.

101
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,580
Maybe the fire helped.

102
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:50,960
Maybe he was just lucky.

103
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:53,520
Oh, and that creepy bird mask you're imagining?

104
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:55,480
The plague doctor with the long beak?

105
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:56,220
Not here.

106
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:00,800
That costume shows up in the 1600s, about three hundred years later.

107
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:05,120
In 1348, the person treating you looks like...

108
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:05,820
a guy.

109
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:08,140
Probably a very nervous guy.

110
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:12,560
We're about halfway through, and your odds are not looking great.

111
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:16,720
But remember: if you make it to the end, things get weirdly good.

112
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:17,990
First, though...

113
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:19,020
it gets worse.

114
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:21,060
So the doctors can't help.

115
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:22,220
What about the Church?

116
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:26,620
For you, in 1348, the Church is everything.

117
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:29,480
And the worst thing that can happen isn't dying.

118
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:31,740
It's dying without confession.

119
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:32,980
Here's the problem.

120
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:34,720
Priests visit the sick.

121
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:36,340
Priests catch the plague.

122
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:37,800
Priests die.

123
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:47,020
So many that in January 1349, the Bishop of Bath and Wells sent an emergency message to his diocese: if no priest can be found,

124
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:49,960
the dying can confess to an ordinary person.

125
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:52,060
And if no man is available...

126
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:53,140
even to a woman.

127
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,660
For the medieval Church, that was a five-alarm fire.

128
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:58,080
Nobody is safe.

129
00:05:58,340 --> 00:05:59,960
Not even the top of society.

130
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:06,040
In 1348, the King's own daughter, Princess Joan, died of plague near Bordeaux,

131
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:08,380
on her way to marry a Spanish prince.

132
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:13,080
In 1349, the man chosen to be Archbishop of Canterbury died.

133
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:17,300
Then his replacement died too, just weeks after taking the job.

134
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:20,060
In London, the churchyards filled up.

135
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:25,440
So the city opened new emergency cemeteries, with long trenches for the dead.

136
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:31,220
In 2011, scientists pulled the plague's DNA from skeletons buried at one of them,

137
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:32,040
East Smithfield.

138
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:36,880
More than six hundred and fifty years later, the culprit was finally confirmed.

139
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:39,400
So, what can you actually do?

140
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:40,700
Let's look at your options.

141
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:42,420
Option one: run.

142
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:43,680
That's what the rich did.

143
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:51,140
In Florence, the writer Giovanni Boccaccio described wealthy families leaving the city for their country houses and waiting it out.

144
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:51,980
Great plan.

145
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:53,600
If you have a country house.

146
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,420
You don't.

147
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:56,420
You're tied to your lord's land.

148
00:06:56,740 --> 00:06:59,700
Leaving without permission can get you into serious trouble.

149
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:02,520
And even if you did leave, where would you go?

150
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:05,250
Every village is dealing with the same thing.

151
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:08,880
And the plague is usually already there, waiting for you.

152
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:11,220
Option two: pray harder.

153
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:18,580
Across Europe, groups called flagellants marched from town to town, whipping themselves in public to beg God for mercy.

154
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:24,840
In 1349, a group of them crossed over from Holland and Zeeland and put on a show in London.

155
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,520
The Londoners watched.

156
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:28,640
Mostly, they did not join in.

157
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:29,720
Very English.

158
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:33,520
Option three, and this is the dark one: blame someone.

159
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:39,300
In many parts of Europe, terrified people blamed Jewish communities for poisoning the wells,

160
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:42,040
and thousands of innocent people were murdered.

161
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:45,800
In England, there were no Jewish communities left to blame.

162
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,140
They had been expelled in 1290.

163
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:55,920
It's one of the most shameful chapters of the whole pandemic, and a reminder that fear can be as deadly as any disease.

164
00:07:55,980 --> 00:08:01,920
So: you can't run, praying doesn't seem to work, and blaming people is horrifying.

165
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,180
Which leaves you with option four.

166
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:06,180
Wait.

167
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:07,600
And hope.

168
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:09,980
Be honest: which one would you have picked?

169
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:11,320
Tell me in the comments.

170
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:13,740
So, how bad was it really?

171
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:16,940
Historians still argue about the exact numbers.

172
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:23,180
Medieval record-keeping wasn't great, and it's hard to count when the people counting are also dying.

173
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:29,100
But most estimates for England land somewhere between 40 and 60 percent of the population,

174
00:08:29,100 --> 00:08:31,260
dead, in about two years.

175
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:35,740
Across Europe, estimates range from about a third to more than half.

176
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:37,260
And it's not just people.

177
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:43,669
A chronicler in Leicester, Henry Knighton, wrote that sheep and cattle wandered loose through the fields and the crops,

178
00:08:43,669 --> 00:08:46,220
because there was no one left to drive them.

179
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:48,380
Harvests rotted in the ground.

180
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,540
There simply weren't enough hands.

181
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:51,820
Picture your village.

182
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:52,860
A hundred people.

183
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:53,660
The miller.

184
00:08:53,660 --> 00:08:54,620
The blacksmith.

185
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:55,580
Your neighbors.

186
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:58,940
The annoying guy who always borrows your plow without asking.

187
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:02,060
By 1350, around half of them are gone.

188
00:09:03,020 --> 00:09:04,050
Statistically...

189
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:05,500
so are you.

190
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:07,180
But let's say you're lucky.

191
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:09,580
Let's say you're one of the people who make it.

192
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:11,180
What's your life like now?

193
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:12,000
Weirdly...

194
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:12,860
better.

195
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:13,820
Think about it.

196
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:15,500
Half the workers are gone.

197
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,500
The fields still need plowing.

198
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:21,260
Suddenly, your lord needs you more than you need him.

199
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:23,980
So peasants start asking for higher wages.

200
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:24,940
And they get them.

201
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:27,260
The people in charge hate this.

202
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:36,220
In 1349, King Edward the Third issued the Ordinance of Labourers, and in 1351 Parliament passed the Statute of Labourers.

203
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:40,040
Both tried to force wages back to what they were before the plague,

204
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:42,540
and punished anyone who refused to work.

205
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:44,060
It didn't really work.

206
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:45,820
Wages kept creeping up.

207
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:48,060
Workers moved to wherever paid better.

208
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:56,140
And over the following generations, many ordinary people ate more meat, drank more ale and lived better than their parents ever had.

209
00:09:56,140 --> 00:10:02,220
About thirty years later, in 1381, all that tension exploded into the Peasants' Revolt.

210
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:04,460
But that's a story for another video.

211
00:10:04,460 --> 00:10:06,700
So congratulations, again.

212
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:09,260
You probably wouldn't survive the Black Death.

213
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:10,380
But if you did...

214
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:13,340
you might finally get something more than bread.

215
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:15,180
Oh, and one last thing.

216
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:16,700
"Ring Around the Rosie"?

217
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:17,820
Not about the plague.

218
00:10:17,820 --> 00:10:25,740
The rhyme first appeared in print in 1881, and folklorists only connected it to the plague in the twentieth century.

219
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:26,540
Sorry.

220
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:31,260
If you want to know what it was like to be the person who walked into plague houses on purpose,

221
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:33,180
the next video is right here.

222
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:38,540
And subscribe, because this little guy in the red scarf has a lot more centuries to suffer through.
